00:03:25.880 --> 00:03:28.830
OK, so we'll get started,then.

00:03:28.830 --> 00:03:31.280
So my name is Carly.

00:03:31.280 --> 00:03:34.350
I think that whenever you're
listening to a presentation,

00:03:34.350 --> 00:03:37.890
you should get context for where
all of this is coming from.

00:03:37.890 --> 00:03:40.100
There's no hard and fast
rules in entrepreneurship,

00:03:40.100 --> 00:03:41.960
and there is no
single framework.

00:03:41.960 --> 00:03:43.963
So therefore,
everything in my opinion

00:03:43.963 --> 00:03:46.130
is an opinion that you're
going to hear from anybody

00:03:46.130 --> 00:03:46.963
in entrepreneurship.

00:03:46.963 --> 00:03:48.980
It's just based on
their own experiences,

00:03:48.980 --> 00:03:52.700
and the range of experiences of
entrepreneurs is really vast.

00:03:52.700 --> 00:03:55.550
And so the context--

00:03:55.550 --> 00:03:57.590
and to basically
contextualize what

00:03:57.590 --> 00:04:00.240
I'm going to tell you today
is based on my own experience.

00:04:00.240 --> 00:04:03.680
So I want to give you
the full context on me.

00:04:03.680 --> 00:04:08.420
So I started my career at
Goldman Sachs in finance.

00:04:08.420 --> 00:04:11.150
I lived through the 2008
financial crisis there.

00:04:11.150 --> 00:04:13.430
Stayed a number of
years after that,

00:04:13.430 --> 00:04:17.047
and then decided that the
corporate world which I thought

00:04:17.047 --> 00:04:19.130
was going to be my entire
career, that I was going

00:04:19.130 --> 00:04:21.500
to try something else on,
that maybe this wasn't

00:04:21.500 --> 00:04:25.370
going to be the most fun place
for the rest of my career.

00:04:25.370 --> 00:04:28.280
And decided to go join
the local government

00:04:28.280 --> 00:04:30.140
in New York City,
where I actually live

00:04:30.140 --> 00:04:31.040
and still live today.

00:04:31.040 --> 00:04:33.020
I'm in Brooklyn right now.

00:04:33.020 --> 00:04:36.470
So I worked for Mayor Michael
Bloomberg's administration

00:04:36.470 --> 00:04:37.520
in economic development.

00:04:37.520 --> 00:04:40.490
And back then, this was
about eight years ago,

00:04:40.490 --> 00:04:41.960
New York was trying
to figure out

00:04:41.960 --> 00:04:44.720
how to compete after
the financial crisis,

00:04:44.720 --> 00:04:46.940
how to diversify our
economy, and one way

00:04:46.940 --> 00:04:48.950
was to bring more
startups to New York.

00:04:48.950 --> 00:04:50.450
So we were trying
to figure out, how

00:04:50.450 --> 00:04:53.870
do we compete with San
Francisco and London and Boston,

00:04:53.870 --> 00:04:56.690
and make New York more
attractive for founders.

00:04:56.690 --> 00:05:01.050
The arts was a huge existing
industry in New York,

00:05:01.050 --> 00:05:01.620
for instance.

00:05:01.620 --> 00:05:04.508
So we said, what are the
assets that we already have?

00:05:04.508 --> 00:05:06.050
And how can we get
more entrepreneurs

00:05:06.050 --> 00:05:08.060
to feel confident that
New York is a great place

00:05:08.060 --> 00:05:10.680
to not only start, but be
able to grow their business?

00:05:10.680 --> 00:05:12.950
So I worked with
a lot of founders.

00:05:12.950 --> 00:05:17.292
Sort of figured out what the
perceived and real challenges

00:05:17.292 --> 00:05:19.250
were with coming to New
York, and that's really

00:05:19.250 --> 00:05:21.500
how I got my startup bug.

00:05:21.500 --> 00:05:26.990
So then I decided to really
try out startups and was hired

00:05:26.990 --> 00:05:29.240
as the general manager of a
real estate tech startup--

00:05:29.240 --> 00:05:32.510
another industry that's
enormous in New York--

00:05:32.510 --> 00:05:36.350
real estate, and was the
founder of the New York

00:05:36.350 --> 00:05:39.322
office for a real estate
market place called PivotDesk.

00:05:39.322 --> 00:05:41.030
I did that for a number
of years until we

00:05:41.030 --> 00:05:44.330
were sold to a national
co-working provider called

00:05:44.330 --> 00:05:45.110
Industrious.

00:05:45.110 --> 00:05:48.830
And then since have been
within either a full-time or

00:05:48.830 --> 00:05:51.590
consulting capacity, and now I
advise a couple of early stage

00:05:51.590 --> 00:05:54.080
startups, a number of
venture-backed startups,

00:05:54.080 --> 00:05:56.630
and also have my own
non-venture-backed startup,

00:05:56.630 --> 00:05:59.498
which is another option
for people who are starting

00:05:59.498 --> 00:06:00.290
their own business.

00:06:00.290 --> 00:06:03.830
They don't always have to do
the full venture-backed crazy

00:06:03.830 --> 00:06:06.770
accelerated growth business
model called Crabwalk,

00:06:06.770 --> 00:06:10.070
which is all about helping
people get ownership over

00:06:10.070 --> 00:06:14.360
their careers and this
new way of work where--

00:06:14.360 --> 00:06:15.920
I thought, like I
already mentioned,

00:06:15.920 --> 00:06:16.670
I was at Goldman Sachs.

00:06:16.670 --> 00:06:18.350
I thought I was going to be
there and go up a ladder.

00:06:18.350 --> 00:06:20.767
There is no ladder anymore,
so people need a lot more help

00:06:20.767 --> 00:06:22.220
in navigating their careers.

00:06:22.220 --> 00:06:24.860
And in between that all,
I found myself here at MIT

00:06:24.860 --> 00:06:28.040
where I have been for about 3
and 1/2 years an entrepreneur

00:06:28.040 --> 00:06:30.620
in residence at the
Trust Center running

00:06:30.620 --> 00:06:32.790
a number of our
extracurricular programs,

00:06:32.790 --> 00:06:35.450
including our accelerator
program, delta v and our Intro

00:06:35.450 --> 00:06:37.670
to Entrepreneurship
bootcamp [INAUDIBLE] MIT.

00:06:37.670 --> 00:06:42.545
I also am a senior lecturer
for two classes, and as an EIR

00:06:42.545 --> 00:06:44.940
get to coach a
bunch of students,

00:06:44.940 --> 00:06:46.400
which is a ton of fun.

00:06:46.400 --> 00:06:49.490
So since we're going to be
talking about business plans,

00:06:49.490 --> 00:06:50.900
I will tell you that I've seen--

00:06:50.900 --> 00:06:52.430
I've had the privilege of
seeing so many business

00:06:52.430 --> 00:06:54.890
plans at this point, both
from early in my career

00:06:54.890 --> 00:06:58.520
and now at MIT talking to
lots of different students

00:06:58.520 --> 00:07:01.920
who work on lots of different
industries and problems.

00:07:01.920 --> 00:07:07.280
And if you guys could
tell me for anybody

00:07:07.280 --> 00:07:09.050
who wants to respond,
I'm just curious

00:07:09.050 --> 00:07:10.612
what you hear by
a business plan,

00:07:10.612 --> 00:07:12.570
because I feel like this
is a really big topic.

00:07:12.570 --> 00:07:14.090
There's business
plan competitions,

00:07:14.090 --> 00:07:15.770
there's business
plan submissions,

00:07:15.770 --> 00:07:17.690
there's financial projections.

00:07:17.690 --> 00:07:19.940
I'm just curious any
perceptions that people

00:07:19.940 --> 00:07:22.790
have about what is this
business plan thing anyways.

00:07:28.330 --> 00:07:32.410
I can show you what I
tend to think of it as.

00:07:32.410 --> 00:07:37.900
It's your go-forward plan for
how you intend to make revenue

00:07:37.900 --> 00:07:39.680
through what strings.

00:07:39.680 --> 00:07:42.530
How you intend to be
a viable business.

00:07:42.530 --> 00:07:44.800
And not only viable
at point of launch,

00:07:44.800 --> 00:07:47.300
but also sustainable
going forward.

00:07:47.300 --> 00:07:49.500
Yeah, I really like that.

00:07:49.500 --> 00:07:53.080
So I agree that a lot
of business plans are--

00:07:53.080 --> 00:07:56.800
when I think of business plan,
I also put it adjacent to money

00:07:56.800 --> 00:07:59.710
and I put it adjacent to
profits and profitability.

00:07:59.710 --> 00:08:02.770
And the thing about a businesses
is that is the ultimate goal.

00:08:02.770 --> 00:08:04.360
You do not have a
business unless you

00:08:04.360 --> 00:08:05.277
have paying customers.

00:08:05.277 --> 00:08:08.200
That is a core role of
ours at the trust center.

00:08:08.200 --> 00:08:11.020
But I really think about
the business plan as helpful

00:08:11.020 --> 00:08:11.900
in a lot of ways.

00:08:11.900 --> 00:08:12.850
And so that's really--

00:08:12.850 --> 00:08:17.050
that's the end goal and what
Sida really hit on the head

00:08:17.050 --> 00:08:20.592
is we need a direction, right?

00:08:20.592 --> 00:08:22.300
A lot of times in
entrepreneurship things

00:08:22.300 --> 00:08:23.800
are going so fast,
and you just want

00:08:23.800 --> 00:08:26.500
to get to the next goal
and the next milestone

00:08:26.500 --> 00:08:27.520
and the next milestone.

00:08:27.520 --> 00:08:29.750
But if you don't actually
have a direction,

00:08:29.750 --> 00:08:32.030
if you don't know where
you're going in the long run,

00:08:32.030 --> 00:08:35.530
you're going to
miss a lot of stuff.

00:08:35.530 --> 00:08:38.409
Your vision will probably
actually narrow down smaller

00:08:38.409 --> 00:08:40.600
to what it really could
be if you took a moment

00:08:40.600 --> 00:08:45.010
to look around and
understand the full picture

00:08:45.010 --> 00:08:47.500
and set a direction yourself.

00:08:47.500 --> 00:08:52.660
So I really love this quote that
Brad Feld, who actually happens

00:08:52.660 --> 00:08:55.150
to be an MIT grad, and as a
really prominent and famous

00:08:55.150 --> 00:08:58.220
venture capitalist, I
have a lot of respect for.

00:08:58.220 --> 00:08:59.770
And he says, it's
a critical document

00:08:59.770 --> 00:09:01.930
for an entrepreneur to
organize their thoughts

00:09:01.930 --> 00:09:03.970
around their new business.

00:09:03.970 --> 00:09:05.990
And this is what I think
is really critical.

00:09:05.990 --> 00:09:09.400
So I think see that you really
hit the nail on the head

00:09:09.400 --> 00:09:11.500
in terms of what the
ultimate goal is.

00:09:11.500 --> 00:09:15.190
It is to get revenue and to
create a sustainable business.

00:09:15.190 --> 00:09:17.020
Because without
customers and revenue,

00:09:17.020 --> 00:09:19.240
there is no
sustainable business.

00:09:19.240 --> 00:09:21.910
But I really think, and I
really love this phrasing,

00:09:21.910 --> 00:09:24.700
that a business plan is an
opportunity for you to really

00:09:24.700 --> 00:09:26.270
organize your thoughts.

00:09:26.270 --> 00:09:29.723
And to me, one of the
biggest goals or reasons

00:09:29.723 --> 00:09:31.390
why you'd want to
organize your thoughts

00:09:31.390 --> 00:09:33.400
is so you can communicate them.

00:09:33.400 --> 00:09:35.380
Because really, your
job as an entrepreneur

00:09:35.380 --> 00:09:37.300
at the end of the day
is to set a vision

00:09:37.300 --> 00:09:38.690
and communicate your vision.

00:09:38.690 --> 00:09:39.190
Right?

00:09:39.190 --> 00:09:41.390
That's so you can raise
money from investors

00:09:41.390 --> 00:09:44.710
and so you can find teammates
and have co-founders

00:09:44.710 --> 00:09:46.240
and eventually employees.

00:09:46.240 --> 00:09:49.120
It's so you can talk to
partners and customers.

00:09:49.120 --> 00:09:51.140
Really having a way to
organize your thoughts

00:09:51.140 --> 00:09:54.250
so you can communicate them
out is really, really key.

00:09:54.250 --> 00:09:56.830
And frankly, it's something
that I really see as undervalued

00:09:56.830 --> 00:09:57.940
in most entrepreneurs.

00:09:57.940 --> 00:10:01.240
They just think the product is
the important thing to refine,

00:10:01.240 --> 00:10:05.110
and I'll just wing my
messaging or the way I pitch.

00:10:05.110 --> 00:10:09.160
And that is really, I would
almost say, the opposite.

00:10:09.160 --> 00:10:11.067
Good product is a must have.

00:10:11.067 --> 00:10:13.150
You don't really have a
business if you don't have

00:10:13.150 --> 00:10:14.930
something exciting to sell.

00:10:14.930 --> 00:10:16.690
But if you can't
communicate that out,

00:10:16.690 --> 00:10:19.660
it really doesn't matter
in the first place.

00:10:19.660 --> 00:10:21.610
And just piggybacking
off of that,

00:10:21.610 --> 00:10:24.280
it's one of the
elemental aspects

00:10:24.280 --> 00:10:26.410
of the $15K specifically.

00:10:26.410 --> 00:10:30.190
I think that it's necessary
to have bedrock business

00:10:30.190 --> 00:10:32.800
fundamentals, but
especially in the arts.

00:10:32.800 --> 00:10:35.800
We don't have as many
quantitative measures

00:10:35.800 --> 00:10:38.650
to be able to convey
in a slide, and I

00:10:38.650 --> 00:10:41.470
think that while pitches
are really important,

00:10:41.470 --> 00:10:44.740
I think that we really
emphasize and value

00:10:44.740 --> 00:10:48.610
a really clear communicative,
persuasive, and engaging pitch

00:10:48.610 --> 00:10:50.170
maybe above other--

00:10:50.170 --> 00:10:52.300
almost anything else.

00:10:52.300 --> 00:10:53.870
Yeah, totally.

00:10:53.870 --> 00:10:58.270
And so these are a few
reasons that I came up

00:10:58.270 --> 00:11:02.290
to give you a whole set
of reasons as to why you

00:11:02.290 --> 00:11:05.080
do a business plan,
but I would actually

00:11:05.080 --> 00:11:09.310
move your eyes to the third
one in the middle of the slide.

00:11:09.310 --> 00:11:11.710
Because this is really how I
think about a business plan.

00:11:11.710 --> 00:11:15.190
It's like this master document
you have that helps you.

00:11:15.190 --> 00:11:16.312
It's your business plan--

00:11:16.312 --> 00:11:17.770
this version of
your business plan,

00:11:17.770 --> 00:11:18.820
at least, you're
going to have lots

00:11:18.820 --> 00:11:20.195
of versions of
your business plan

00:11:20.195 --> 00:11:22.540
depending on who
the audience is.

00:11:22.540 --> 00:11:25.630
And we can talk about what the
different audiences you'll need

00:11:25.630 --> 00:11:27.190
it for, I just mentioned a few.

00:11:27.190 --> 00:11:29.980
But think of this plan that
I'm talking about today

00:11:29.980 --> 00:11:33.130
as your master document that's
an internal document that's

00:11:33.130 --> 00:11:34.390
just for you.

00:11:34.390 --> 00:11:36.340
It's a living
document that you're

00:11:36.340 --> 00:11:38.860
going to update all
the time and iterate on

00:11:38.860 --> 00:11:41.080
so that you can pull
from it when you're

00:11:41.080 --> 00:11:42.620
talking to a specific audience.

00:11:42.620 --> 00:11:44.680
So, OK, here's my master plan.

00:11:44.680 --> 00:11:47.470
It has 15 different sections.

00:11:47.470 --> 00:11:49.987
When I'm talking to an
investor versus a customer,

00:11:49.987 --> 00:11:51.820
I'm going to need to
pull different sections

00:11:51.820 --> 00:11:52.870
and insights from it.

00:11:52.870 --> 00:11:55.552
Not everybody should see
everything about your business.

00:11:55.552 --> 00:11:57.010
And you should be
really thoughtful

00:11:57.010 --> 00:11:59.980
and you'll learn how to do that
over time about what to share

00:11:59.980 --> 00:12:02.090
and frankly, what not to share.

00:12:02.090 --> 00:12:04.090
And that's a whole other
different communication

00:12:04.090 --> 00:12:07.310
skill set that we could talk
about at a different time.

00:12:07.310 --> 00:12:10.270
And so I would argue I think
a lot of business plans

00:12:10.270 --> 00:12:13.210
are talked about in
PowerPoint or even Excel

00:12:13.210 --> 00:12:16.090
when you think about
financial projections.

00:12:16.090 --> 00:12:19.390
I would argue unless you love
PowerPoint, and some people do,

00:12:19.390 --> 00:12:22.250
I personally don't--
this is even keynote,

00:12:22.250 --> 00:12:25.490
a better version of PowerPoint
in my opinion that I use today.

00:12:25.490 --> 00:12:27.670
But I really like
text, especially when

00:12:27.670 --> 00:12:29.440
we're talking communication.

00:12:29.440 --> 00:12:32.950
I think it's so important to
be able to write succinctly

00:12:32.950 --> 00:12:34.743
and compellingly in text.

00:12:34.743 --> 00:12:36.160
Because that's how
we communicate.

00:12:36.160 --> 00:12:41.290
We talk on the phone, we write
emails, we write proposals.

00:12:41.290 --> 00:12:44.830
And PowerPoint is one way to
communicate, but in my head

00:12:44.830 --> 00:12:48.920
it's not the world's best way
to get out all of your thoughts.

00:12:48.920 --> 00:12:51.220
It could be a high level
version of your thoughts.

00:12:51.220 --> 00:12:52.600
And you can take
that master text

00:12:52.600 --> 00:12:55.000
and translate it into PowerPoint
for different audiences.

00:12:55.000 --> 00:12:58.630
But again, my two cents,
my personal opinion,

00:12:58.630 --> 00:13:01.120
my personal experience,
I don't necessarily

00:13:01.120 --> 00:13:06.440
think PowerPoint is the way to
communicate your business plan.

00:13:06.440 --> 00:13:08.590
So again, we talked about
this a little before,

00:13:08.590 --> 00:13:10.960
but who is a business plan for?

00:13:10.960 --> 00:13:15.370
I just put a scenario out here,
and for Sida, you to could even

00:13:15.370 --> 00:13:17.687
say just one co-founder now.

00:13:17.687 --> 00:13:20.020
Say you're two co-founders,
you started working together

00:13:20.020 --> 00:13:22.180
in September at the
beginning of the school year,

00:13:22.180 --> 00:13:24.340
and you want to recruit
other team members

00:13:24.340 --> 00:13:26.200
and enter competitions soon.

00:13:26.200 --> 00:13:28.180
Maybe you've done some
primary market research,

00:13:28.180 --> 00:13:29.990
you've begun to prototype.

00:13:29.990 --> 00:13:32.090
So we need a master document.

00:13:32.090 --> 00:13:34.630
So we're two co-founders,
or I'm one person

00:13:34.630 --> 00:13:36.310
that works by myself.

00:13:36.310 --> 00:13:41.950
Or maybe I'm-- a typical
scenario is somebody,

00:13:41.950 --> 00:13:45.220
maybe a professor or
PI and two students,

00:13:45.220 --> 00:13:47.230
working to
commercialize research.

00:13:47.230 --> 00:13:50.170
And what this document
can do for you

00:13:50.170 --> 00:13:53.200
as co-founders, or team members,
or for a single person who's

00:13:53.200 --> 00:13:54.650
working on their business.

00:13:54.650 --> 00:13:57.710
This is just an opportunity
to coalesce your thoughts,

00:13:57.710 --> 00:14:01.440
and to write down everything
that's happening today.

00:14:01.440 --> 00:14:04.090
It's a what's happened
up to now document.

00:14:04.090 --> 00:14:06.003
And where do I want
to go from here?

00:14:06.003 --> 00:14:07.420
And again, you're
going to iterate

00:14:07.420 --> 00:14:09.697
this, and iterate, and
iterate, and refine it

00:14:09.697 --> 00:14:11.530
depending on the audience
you're talking to.

00:14:11.530 --> 00:14:15.925
But this is almost one
of the first things

00:14:15.925 --> 00:14:17.800
you should do in your
business in my opinion.

00:14:17.800 --> 00:14:21.190
Because-- especially when you
have multiple people involved.

00:14:21.190 --> 00:14:23.890
This is also a great document
if you do have a co-founder

00:14:23.890 --> 00:14:25.900
or you do have team
members, to also

00:14:25.900 --> 00:14:27.880
make sure you guys
are on the same page.

00:14:27.880 --> 00:14:29.680
It's really easy to
assume that you're

00:14:29.680 --> 00:14:32.890
going in the same direction, but
if you don't actually check in

00:14:32.890 --> 00:14:35.290
and confirm that you
could easily be diverging.

00:14:35.290 --> 00:14:37.670
And so the earlier in your
venture you can do this,

00:14:37.670 --> 00:14:42.270
the more aligned you're
going to be in the long run.

00:14:42.270 --> 00:14:44.900
So when we think about what
should be in my business plan--

00:14:44.900 --> 00:14:48.740
and this is going to be
a really long slide--

00:14:48.740 --> 00:14:51.857
but we can talk too, and
we can talk to whatever

00:14:51.857 --> 00:14:52.940
people want to talk about.

00:14:52.940 --> 00:14:55.190
But these are suggestions.

00:14:55.190 --> 00:14:57.230
These are what I've
seen in business plans.

00:14:57.230 --> 00:15:01.010
And you might be able to fill
in all of these sections today,

00:15:01.010 --> 00:15:02.510
and you might not be able to.

00:15:02.510 --> 00:15:05.930
And I think as much as
you need to understand

00:15:05.930 --> 00:15:08.350
and comprehend what
you currently have,

00:15:08.350 --> 00:15:10.730
it's also an opportunity to
figure out what don't I have?

00:15:10.730 --> 00:15:11.938
What haven't I thought about?

00:15:11.938 --> 00:15:13.370
Or what haven't I
figured out yet?

00:15:13.370 --> 00:15:16.670
Or what am I less confident in?

00:15:16.670 --> 00:15:19.400
And again, the benefit
of putting this in text

00:15:19.400 --> 00:15:22.860
is you can also use this
document to get feedback.

00:15:22.860 --> 00:15:25.850
One thing that entrepreneurs
need to be good at

00:15:25.850 --> 00:15:27.890
is asking for help.

00:15:27.890 --> 00:15:30.740
And so not only
asking for help, like

00:15:30.740 --> 00:15:33.290
I'm really bad at
creating a vision.

00:15:33.290 --> 00:15:34.860
That's something that scares me.

00:15:34.860 --> 00:15:37.790
I'm really good at saying in
the near term what I want,

00:15:37.790 --> 00:15:40.550
but what is that really-- what
could that be in the long term?

00:15:40.550 --> 00:15:45.620
Or I'm less great at
understanding unit economics,

00:15:45.620 --> 00:15:47.380
can I get some feedback on this?

00:15:47.380 --> 00:15:49.610
This business plan
hopefully empowers

00:15:49.610 --> 00:15:53.630
you to go and get the advice
and the support you need.

00:15:53.630 --> 00:15:57.207
And also to be able to check in
and say this makes sense to me,

00:15:57.207 --> 00:15:58.790
does this make sense
to somebody else?

00:15:58.790 --> 00:16:02.570
And that overarching
goal of communication.

00:16:02.570 --> 00:16:04.880
And again, this is just
a list of suggestions

00:16:04.880 --> 00:16:07.550
that you should absolutely
customize to your business

00:16:07.550 --> 00:16:09.930
and your stage of business.

00:16:09.930 --> 00:16:13.868
If you're that scenario
that I just put before this,

00:16:13.868 --> 00:16:16.160
it's probably pretty hard to
have financial projections

00:16:16.160 --> 00:16:16.743
at this point.

00:16:16.743 --> 00:16:17.690
And that's totally OK.

00:16:17.690 --> 00:16:20.480
Just leave it open, and
know that every time

00:16:20.480 --> 00:16:25.070
you get a clue for that section
just add it add it back in.

00:16:25.070 --> 00:16:27.930
OK, so just to go through
these really quick.

00:16:27.930 --> 00:16:31.550
Just going to switch
where my Zoom is so

00:16:31.550 --> 00:16:34.010
I can see every single bullet.

00:16:34.010 --> 00:16:37.530
So starting with your mission
statement and your vision

00:16:37.530 --> 00:16:38.030
statement.

00:16:38.030 --> 00:16:39.830
And I think there's a
big question sometimes

00:16:39.830 --> 00:16:41.455
about the difference
between these two.

00:16:41.455 --> 00:16:43.910
To me, a vision is
really the direction

00:16:43.910 --> 00:16:45.740
you want to go in long term.

00:16:45.740 --> 00:16:47.570
Maybe it's not--
has nothing to do

00:16:47.570 --> 00:16:49.280
with the way you're
going to go to market

00:16:49.280 --> 00:16:50.390
or your first customer.

00:16:50.390 --> 00:16:52.370
It's really how do I
see this business having

00:16:52.370 --> 00:16:55.010
impact in the long run?

00:16:55.010 --> 00:16:57.320
What is the ultimate
goal of this business?

00:16:57.320 --> 00:16:59.780
And the mission statement
is really close to that.

00:16:59.780 --> 00:17:02.120
But that's almost
why am I doing this?

00:17:02.120 --> 00:17:04.260
Or why are we doing
this as a team?

00:17:04.260 --> 00:17:05.960
Why is this important
in the world?

00:17:05.960 --> 00:17:06.859
Or in my industry?

00:17:06.859 --> 00:17:08.690
Or to my customer?

00:17:08.690 --> 00:17:11.810
So vision, or the direction
you want to go, and mission,

00:17:11.810 --> 00:17:15.240
I would say why
this is important.

00:17:15.240 --> 00:17:17.210
The next most important thing.

00:17:17.210 --> 00:17:19.369
What is the problem
you're solving?

00:17:19.369 --> 00:17:23.150
So, so important to be
able to succinctly state.

00:17:23.150 --> 00:17:25.490
This is actually sometimes
one of the hardest things

00:17:25.490 --> 00:17:29.990
to do because you
start with a problem,

00:17:29.990 --> 00:17:32.540
and then you decide to go
talk to a bunch of customers.

00:17:32.540 --> 00:17:35.542
And then you sort
of pivot from where

00:17:35.542 --> 00:17:38.000
you started to more of what
you're hearing in the customers

00:17:38.000 --> 00:17:39.170
and back again.

00:17:39.170 --> 00:17:43.280
A lot of times all of the
different customer research

00:17:43.280 --> 00:17:47.480
we do, and market research we
do, creates a maze in our heads

00:17:47.480 --> 00:17:48.290
as entrepreneurs.

00:17:48.290 --> 00:17:50.210
I don't know if that's
making sense and resonating

00:17:50.210 --> 00:17:50.710
with folks.

00:17:50.710 --> 00:17:54.290
That typically does when
I'm teaching to my students.

00:17:54.290 --> 00:17:59.435
And again, taking an opportunity
to really try to write out

00:17:59.435 --> 00:18:00.560
the problem you're solving.

00:18:00.560 --> 00:18:03.060
It might sound trivial, but
it's actually, in my opinion,

00:18:03.060 --> 00:18:06.440
one of the hardest parts
of the business plan.

00:18:06.440 --> 00:18:09.140
Then you want to talk about
all of the market research

00:18:09.140 --> 00:18:09.830
you've done.

00:18:09.830 --> 00:18:13.430
So I've talked to
these many customers,

00:18:13.430 --> 00:18:15.548
or prospective customers.

00:18:15.548 --> 00:18:17.090
I've researched the
market landscape.

00:18:17.090 --> 00:18:19.730
I understand the history of the
problem I'm trying to solve.

00:18:19.730 --> 00:18:22.370
I understand the context
I'm trying to operate in.

00:18:22.370 --> 00:18:24.620
I understand those
who are currently

00:18:24.620 --> 00:18:26.330
trying to solve this problem.

00:18:26.330 --> 00:18:29.660
Here is who's tried it in
the past and why they failed.

00:18:29.660 --> 00:18:32.450
And then you go on
to your solution.

00:18:32.450 --> 00:18:34.520
Here is the solution to
the problem I'm solving.

00:18:34.520 --> 00:18:40.100
Here is-- if it's a product that
you've prototyped so far, have

00:18:40.100 --> 00:18:43.580
product specs, or a product
brochure, you can put that in.

00:18:43.580 --> 00:18:46.970
Here is the way I'm going to
develop this product over time.

00:18:46.970 --> 00:18:48.860
That'll get to,
eventually, the resources

00:18:48.860 --> 00:18:51.920
that you need to
create this company.

00:18:51.920 --> 00:18:55.190
And then here's how I'm actually
going to put this into market.

00:18:55.190 --> 00:18:57.840
This is how I'm
going to get started.

00:18:57.840 --> 00:19:00.060
Here's the first
customer I started with.

00:19:00.060 --> 00:19:02.510
If you guys are familiar
with the concepts

00:19:02.510 --> 00:19:05.750
of beachhead market and persona.

00:19:05.750 --> 00:19:07.310
Who is my primary customer?

00:19:07.310 --> 00:19:08.930
And how am I going
to reach them?

00:19:08.930 --> 00:19:11.600
Am I going to do traditional--

00:19:11.600 --> 00:19:12.683
is it a consumer product?

00:19:12.683 --> 00:19:14.600
Do I have to do traditional
digital marketing?

00:19:14.600 --> 00:19:16.010
Is that a B2B product?

00:19:16.010 --> 00:19:18.320
Or am I going to have
a direct sales process?

00:19:18.320 --> 00:19:19.430
Is it a medical device?

00:19:19.430 --> 00:19:22.580
Am I going to have licensing
agreements with existing

00:19:22.580 --> 00:19:24.650
distribution partners?

00:19:24.650 --> 00:19:28.850
What's your idea for how
you get started here?

00:19:28.850 --> 00:19:31.135
And then of course
I know that Shannon

00:19:31.135 --> 00:19:33.260
said you guys are going to
have a workshop on value

00:19:33.260 --> 00:19:34.070
propositions.

00:19:34.070 --> 00:19:36.980
I think another-- such
an important piece

00:19:36.980 --> 00:19:38.530
of communication
for your business.

00:19:38.530 --> 00:19:40.280
Really to understand
the value that you're

00:19:40.280 --> 00:19:44.010
delivering, especially
for this primary customer.

00:19:44.010 --> 00:19:47.490
And then eventually, I do think
business model unit economics,

00:19:47.490 --> 00:19:50.912
financial projections,
those probably

00:19:50.912 --> 00:19:52.620
come a little bit
later, just because you

00:19:52.620 --> 00:19:54.590
do need some information
to really understand

00:19:54.590 --> 00:19:55.840
the world you're operating in.

00:19:55.840 --> 00:19:57.510
You might have some
initial assumptions,

00:19:57.510 --> 00:19:59.670
that is great to include.

00:19:59.670 --> 00:20:00.820
And then the team.

00:20:00.820 --> 00:20:03.720
The team is a really important
part of your business plan.

00:20:03.720 --> 00:20:05.190
Who is working on this?

00:20:05.190 --> 00:20:06.390
What are your skill sets?

00:20:06.390 --> 00:20:08.130
What is your
existing experience?

00:20:08.130 --> 00:20:10.500
Why are you interested in this?

00:20:10.500 --> 00:20:13.350
And perhaps you might even
have a couple of blank people.

00:20:13.350 --> 00:20:15.210
Things you need--
people you need to find

00:20:15.210 --> 00:20:17.760
or skill sets you
need to acquire.

00:20:17.760 --> 00:20:21.430
So again, it can be your
existing business plan,

00:20:21.430 --> 00:20:23.910
especially when you're
really early stage,

00:20:23.910 --> 00:20:27.000
because this can really serve
as an internal document for you.

00:20:27.000 --> 00:20:29.490
It can be, here's all
the things we've done

00:20:29.490 --> 00:20:32.070
and here's all the sections
and questions that we still

00:20:32.070 --> 00:20:34.440
need to fill out and answer.

00:20:34.440 --> 00:20:37.290
And then finally, what
this really helps you do

00:20:37.290 --> 00:20:39.870
is figure out what
resources do I have

00:20:39.870 --> 00:20:41.710
and what resources do I need.

00:20:41.710 --> 00:20:44.460
And so if you've actually
mapped all of this out, and this

00:20:44.460 --> 00:20:46.320
is a lot of work
on a single slide.

00:20:46.320 --> 00:20:48.240
I'm not trying to
diminish the level of work

00:20:48.240 --> 00:20:51.690
that this is to create and
write something like this,

00:20:51.690 --> 00:20:54.570
then you can say, OK, here
are the resources I need.

00:20:54.570 --> 00:20:56.310
Here's the type of
financing that I need

00:20:56.310 --> 00:20:57.490
to get this off the ground.

00:20:57.490 --> 00:20:59.100
Here's the team
I need to create.

00:20:59.100 --> 00:21:02.127
Maybe you have a section
of even here's advisors

00:21:02.127 --> 00:21:02.835
I'd love to have.

00:21:07.670 --> 00:21:11.520
Here are random-- whatever the
resource plan that you need.

00:21:11.520 --> 00:21:14.280
Financing is just one of
those types of resource.

00:21:14.280 --> 00:21:18.390
People are another really
important type of resource.

00:21:18.390 --> 00:21:23.640
So this is really, hopefully,
an entire memo to yourself

00:21:23.640 --> 00:21:25.800
to say here's where
I am today and here's

00:21:25.800 --> 00:21:28.360
what I need going forward.

00:21:28.360 --> 00:21:30.180
So I know I just
said a lot, so I just

00:21:30.180 --> 00:21:32.305
want to take a minute in
case there's any questions

00:21:32.305 --> 00:21:34.470
or if anybody wants to
ask specific questions

00:21:34.470 --> 00:21:36.720
about their situation.

00:21:36.720 --> 00:21:39.090
That was a really good
overview, I think.

00:21:39.090 --> 00:21:42.580
And you hit on a
lot of the things.

00:21:42.580 --> 00:21:45.750
Especially almost
doing a gap analysis.

00:21:45.750 --> 00:21:47.910
Here's what we've got,
here's where we want to be.

00:21:47.910 --> 00:21:49.590
And sort of reverse engineering.

00:21:49.590 --> 00:21:50.490
What is it we need?

00:21:50.490 --> 00:21:51.990
Who is it that we need?

00:21:51.990 --> 00:21:54.970
It really helps everybody to
gain some clarity about that.

00:21:54.970 --> 00:21:57.540
So I really think
that was great.

00:22:00.850 --> 00:22:02.514
I have two questions
if that's OK.

00:22:02.514 --> 00:22:05.360
One is specific to the
creative arts competition.

00:22:05.360 --> 00:22:09.670
So I think a lot of our
ideas are pretty early stage.

00:22:09.670 --> 00:22:11.290
What's the
expectation that we'll

00:22:11.290 --> 00:22:14.020
have unit economics and
financial projections

00:22:14.020 --> 00:22:15.670
for the competition?

00:22:15.670 --> 00:22:19.600
We like to see that this
has had some due diligence

00:22:19.600 --> 00:22:20.680
and some consideration.

00:22:20.680 --> 00:22:22.330
We're looking for
real viability,

00:22:22.330 --> 00:22:24.700
but you also don't
have to hand us

00:22:24.700 --> 00:22:28.740
a perfect, comprehensive 10-year
plan, anything like that.

00:22:28.740 --> 00:22:31.330
So if you use these
sorts of workshops,

00:22:31.330 --> 00:22:33.340
we'll also make some
other resources available.

00:22:33.340 --> 00:22:36.460
We'd like to see something
at least preliminary.

00:22:36.460 --> 00:22:38.350
And in the second part
of the competition,

00:22:38.350 --> 00:22:41.680
there's a lot of one on
one mentorship with people.

00:22:41.680 --> 00:22:44.443
And when you apply,
you can also indicate

00:22:44.443 --> 00:22:45.610
we need help with marketing.

00:22:45.610 --> 00:22:47.480
Or we need help
with our slide deck.

00:22:47.480 --> 00:22:49.120
We need help with
a business plan.

00:22:49.120 --> 00:22:51.970
And ideally, you'll be
matched with a mentor who

00:22:51.970 --> 00:22:54.470
will be able to help you
through those sorts of things.

00:22:54.470 --> 00:22:56.800
So tell you what
you're doing right,

00:22:56.800 --> 00:22:59.270
strengthen any muscles that
need a little more attention.

00:22:59.270 --> 00:23:02.770
So having a basic idea
and an understanding

00:23:02.770 --> 00:23:05.080
of how this all fits into
place is really helpful,

00:23:05.080 --> 00:23:08.240
and there will also be more
guidance and resources.

00:23:08.240 --> 00:23:09.000
Got it.

00:23:09.000 --> 00:23:09.630
OK, perfect.

00:23:09.630 --> 00:23:10.410
That's helpful.

00:23:10.410 --> 00:23:13.980
And my second question is for
business plans in general.

00:23:13.980 --> 00:23:19.217
Carly, how often do you think we
should be evolving and updating

00:23:19.217 --> 00:23:19.800
this document?

00:23:19.800 --> 00:23:22.177
Especially if we're
treating it as a master text

00:23:22.177 --> 00:23:23.760
Yeah, I think that's
a great question.

00:23:23.760 --> 00:23:26.052
But I also want to just go
back to your first question,

00:23:26.052 --> 00:23:31.500
too, because I think a
couple ideas for you--

00:23:31.500 --> 00:23:34.140
have you even started to explore
the idea of unit economics

00:23:34.140 --> 00:23:35.400
for your business?

00:23:35.400 --> 00:23:37.180
No, not really.

00:23:37.180 --> 00:23:38.620
[INAUDIBLE] January on that.

00:23:38.620 --> 00:23:39.120
Yeah.

00:23:39.120 --> 00:23:41.580
So Fuse, in particular,
will be a great place.

00:23:41.580 --> 00:23:43.380
So one way for--

00:23:43.380 --> 00:23:46.110
you're a traditional e-commerce
business at its heart?

00:23:46.110 --> 00:23:48.210
So I think the good
part about that

00:23:48.210 --> 00:23:50.770
is that there are
some existing tactics.

00:23:50.770 --> 00:23:52.920
Whether it's putting
up a landing page

00:23:52.920 --> 00:23:54.722
or testing some Instagram ads.

00:23:54.722 --> 00:23:56.430
I don't know if you
have sandbox funding,

00:23:56.430 --> 00:23:59.280
but that could be a really good
use for that type of funding.

00:23:59.280 --> 00:24:03.150
Just to test some ads and
figure out, for instance,

00:24:03.150 --> 00:24:05.012
there's a unit economic--

00:24:05.012 --> 00:24:06.720
I don't know if you're
familiar with it--

00:24:06.720 --> 00:24:09.270
called Cost Per Click.

00:24:09.270 --> 00:24:11.760
So figuring out how
many people are clicking

00:24:11.760 --> 00:24:14.055
on my ad, what was
the cost of that ad,

00:24:14.055 --> 00:24:15.930
and then dividing it by
the number of clicks,

00:24:15.930 --> 00:24:18.720
and then that's just
a starting ground.

00:24:18.720 --> 00:24:22.080
But the other way, just
because you are in a world

00:24:22.080 --> 00:24:23.040
that's pretty known.

00:24:23.040 --> 00:24:25.350
Obviously, you're trying
to do something different.

00:24:25.350 --> 00:24:27.060
But you could
definitely look also

00:24:27.060 --> 00:24:30.237
at other e-commerce or
direct-to-consumer businesses

00:24:30.237 --> 00:24:32.070
when you're doing some
market landscape just

00:24:32.070 --> 00:24:34.830
to see if there's
some comparables,

00:24:34.830 --> 00:24:37.530
whether that's specific
businesses or expected

00:24:37.530 --> 00:24:39.060
averages.

00:24:39.060 --> 00:24:41.040
That stuff changes
all the time, so you

00:24:41.040 --> 00:24:43.530
want to make sure that that
market research is reasonably

00:24:43.530 --> 00:24:44.950
up to date.

00:24:44.950 --> 00:24:48.870
Obviously another new digital
marketing channel, TikTok,

00:24:48.870 --> 00:24:51.120
hasn't been around a lot,
but there's definitely

00:24:51.120 --> 00:24:54.960
some interesting and creative
ways for customer acquisition

00:24:54.960 --> 00:24:56.342
in that front.

00:24:56.342 --> 00:24:57.300
So those are two ideas.

00:24:57.300 --> 00:24:59.220
I think you'll have
a ton if that would

00:24:59.220 --> 00:25:00.688
be a goal of yours in Fuse.

00:25:00.688 --> 00:25:02.730
I think that you could
easily get a bunch of help

00:25:02.730 --> 00:25:06.670
there doing that and
testing some digital ads.

00:25:06.670 --> 00:25:08.815
But the other thing
is there's definitely

00:25:08.815 --> 00:25:10.440
some comps in the
market that you could

00:25:10.440 --> 00:25:12.805
use as a placeholder for now.

00:25:12.805 --> 00:25:14.430
And then, I think in
terms of updating,

00:25:14.430 --> 00:25:15.638
I think it's a good question.

00:25:15.638 --> 00:25:19.680
So is this a document
that you might always

00:25:19.680 --> 00:25:22.590
want to have open
and updating whenever

00:25:22.590 --> 00:25:23.970
you're getting an opportunity?

00:25:23.970 --> 00:25:26.470
Probably, but I don't
know if that's realistic.

00:25:26.470 --> 00:25:29.790
So what I would say is, I
think having it as a calendar

00:25:29.790 --> 00:25:33.690
reminder once a month
or blocking it out

00:25:33.690 --> 00:25:36.390
for two hours just to
look at it I think,

00:25:36.390 --> 00:25:37.860
is a really good thing.

00:25:37.860 --> 00:25:40.350
Not only because I'm sure
at the stage of business

00:25:40.350 --> 00:25:43.330
you're at you're going to have
lots to update, but I think,

00:25:43.330 --> 00:25:46.500
again, it's just a good
document to help you reflect.

00:25:46.500 --> 00:25:49.275
And also help you
say, OK, here's all--

00:25:49.275 --> 00:25:51.150
these are basically
every single bullet point

00:25:51.150 --> 00:25:53.910
is something to figure
out in your business.

00:25:53.910 --> 00:25:55.450
It's like open questions.

00:25:55.450 --> 00:25:59.470
And so if you can even use
this as a reminder to say,

00:25:59.470 --> 00:26:01.440
hey, here's the
10 open questions

00:26:01.440 --> 00:26:04.170
I need to continue to figure
out how to understand.

00:26:04.170 --> 00:26:05.850
Oh, I've spent a lot
of time this month

00:26:05.850 --> 00:26:10.650
on prototyping my website and
doing customer interviews,

00:26:10.650 --> 00:26:13.050
but I forgot to try
that digital ad thing.

00:26:13.050 --> 00:26:15.210
It'll just help
you stay grounded

00:26:15.210 --> 00:26:17.610
and be able to see the big
picture so you're not really

00:26:17.610 --> 00:26:19.410
missing anything
in the near term.

00:26:19.410 --> 00:26:20.980
Does that make sense?

00:26:20.980 --> 00:26:21.700
Yeah, totally.

00:26:21.700 --> 00:26:22.410
That's really helpful.

00:26:22.410 --> 00:26:22.930
Thank you.

00:26:22.930 --> 00:26:25.533
Yeah, yeah.

00:26:25.533 --> 00:26:26.950
And for you, this
is going to be--

00:26:26.950 --> 00:26:29.230
I would also say the
use for you in this is,

00:26:29.230 --> 00:26:30.910
eventually when you
do find a teammate--

00:26:30.910 --> 00:26:32.868
whether that's a co-founder
or just a teammate,

00:26:32.868 --> 00:26:35.110
it doesn't have to be a
co-founder necessarily--

00:26:35.110 --> 00:26:37.097
this is a great way to
get them on your page

00:26:37.097 --> 00:26:39.180
and help them understand
what you've already done.

00:26:42.530 --> 00:26:43.850
And the slide was just--

00:26:43.850 --> 00:26:47.030
I think I demonstrated
that in the prior slide.

00:26:47.030 --> 00:26:48.710
But in my opinion,
your business plan

00:26:48.710 --> 00:26:50.935
should really be
all encompassing.

00:26:50.935 --> 00:26:52.310
So there might be
sections that I

00:26:52.310 --> 00:26:54.440
didn't include
that you might say,

00:26:54.440 --> 00:26:56.960
I want to talk about x or y.

00:26:56.960 --> 00:27:01.730
And so feel free to add
it and to iterate it

00:27:01.730 --> 00:27:04.400
based on that general template.

00:27:04.400 --> 00:27:06.380
And then I do want
to plug this--

00:27:06.380 --> 00:27:08.600
this is the framework that
we use at the Trust Center

00:27:08.600 --> 00:27:09.830
discipline entrepreneurships.

00:27:09.830 --> 00:27:11.497
So if you want a
framework to figure out

00:27:11.497 --> 00:27:12.920
how to answer all
those questions,

00:27:12.920 --> 00:27:15.440
discipline entrepreneurship
is absolutely

00:27:15.440 --> 00:27:17.570
a great one to leverage.

00:27:17.570 --> 00:27:20.150
So the graphic you're seeing
on the left hand of the screen

00:27:20.150 --> 00:27:22.280
is the 24 step framework.

00:27:22.280 --> 00:27:24.770
If you're not familiar
with this entrepreneurship,

00:27:24.770 --> 00:27:27.710
I would number one say it's
an incredibly easy read.

00:27:27.710 --> 00:27:29.510
It's not a traditional
business text.

00:27:29.510 --> 00:27:33.038
It really is not intimidating,
you can read it in a weekend.

00:27:33.038 --> 00:27:35.330
I'm not a person who loves
to read a book on a weekend,

00:27:35.330 --> 00:27:39.050
but I can easily read discipline
entrepreneurship in a weekend.

00:27:39.050 --> 00:27:41.043
And it really does
take you step by step,

00:27:41.043 --> 00:27:43.460
and you'll realize once you're
going through these that it

00:27:43.460 --> 00:27:45.720
becomes much easier to
answer those questions.

00:27:45.720 --> 00:27:50.757
And these are-- the bullet
points on this slide

00:27:50.757 --> 00:27:52.340
are the high level
points that I think

00:27:52.340 --> 00:27:54.950
this framework is really, really
useful to help you answer.

00:27:54.950 --> 00:27:57.020
And by the way, in
the right order,

00:27:57.020 --> 00:27:59.420
you want to understand who
your customer is before

00:27:59.420 --> 00:28:03.500
designing the product for
that customer, for instance.

00:28:03.500 --> 00:28:05.735
Once you understand the
customer and the product,

00:28:05.735 --> 00:28:07.610
then the question is
how is my customer going

00:28:07.610 --> 00:28:09.012
to find this product?

00:28:09.012 --> 00:28:10.220
How am I going to make money?

00:28:10.220 --> 00:28:11.387
What are the unit economics?

00:28:11.387 --> 00:28:13.580
And then eventually,
how do I actually

00:28:13.580 --> 00:28:18.510
scale this for impact
getting back to that vision?

00:28:18.510 --> 00:28:22.070
And so really the last
slide I have again is

00:28:22.070 --> 00:28:24.350
the benefit of doing all
this work because I know

00:28:24.350 --> 00:28:27.380
that a business plan can be a
tremendous amount of writing

00:28:27.380 --> 00:28:29.390
and a tremendous amount of work.

00:28:29.390 --> 00:28:31.430
But it will make a
lot of things easier.

00:28:31.430 --> 00:28:36.110
It's doing the legwork to
help you be more efficient

00:28:36.110 --> 00:28:37.320
in the long run, frankly.

00:28:37.320 --> 00:28:40.190
And so communication, again,
is one of those things

00:28:40.190 --> 00:28:42.530
that it can take
you a ton of time

00:28:42.530 --> 00:28:45.500
to explain to an investor what's
going on in your business,

00:28:45.500 --> 00:28:48.913
or new hire that you just met
that you're super excited about

00:28:48.913 --> 00:28:50.330
and you want to
email immediately.

00:28:50.330 --> 00:28:54.650
So hopefully it's
obvious to see the value

00:28:54.650 --> 00:28:57.830
of putting some real thought
into this business plan memo

00:28:57.830 --> 00:29:01.310
and taking some time to really
build it out so that you can

00:29:01.310 --> 00:29:03.770
react really, really
quick to people

00:29:03.770 --> 00:29:06.290
who you want to
communicate to questions

00:29:06.290 --> 00:29:08.000
that are asked of you.

00:29:08.000 --> 00:29:12.030
I think about it as a cheat
sheet for my business.

00:29:12.030 --> 00:29:15.270
So that is all I
have for you guys.

00:29:15.270 --> 00:29:17.990
I hope that was helpful
and happy to stick around

00:29:17.990 --> 00:29:19.790
for questions.

00:29:19.790 --> 00:29:23.390
But I will also say we have a
ton of resources on our website

00:29:23.390 --> 00:29:26.870
entrepreneurship.mit.edu
and Orbit, which

00:29:26.870 --> 00:29:28.070
has a huge knowledge base.

00:29:28.070 --> 00:29:30.270
So if you want more
resources in the meantime,

00:29:30.270 --> 00:29:32.230
that's a great place to go to.

00:29:32.230 --> 00:29:33.320
Thank you so much.

00:29:33.320 --> 00:29:34.310
This was great.

00:29:34.310 --> 00:29:37.790
It was so straight forward
and well conceived.

00:29:37.790 --> 00:29:40.815
I think this will
be really helpful.

00:29:40.815 --> 00:29:42.190
Does anybody else
have questions?

00:29:42.190 --> 00:29:43.565
I have a few things
that came up,

00:29:43.565 --> 00:29:47.630
but Sida, you have
any questions?

00:29:47.630 --> 00:29:49.370
I have a question
for Carly that's

00:29:49.370 --> 00:29:51.280
not specific to business
plans so maybe she--

00:29:51.280 --> 00:29:51.780
Yeah, yeah.

00:29:51.780 --> 00:29:52.300
Go for it.

00:29:52.300 --> 00:29:52.850
Want to go first?

00:29:52.850 --> 00:29:53.350
Go for it.

00:29:53.350 --> 00:29:54.120
Go for it.

00:29:54.120 --> 00:29:54.620
OK.

00:29:54.620 --> 00:29:56.660
So Carly, you
mentioned that Crabwalk

00:29:56.660 --> 00:29:58.940
is a venture-backed startup.

00:29:58.940 --> 00:30:01.903
So curious to get your thoughts
on what are the considerations

00:30:01.903 --> 00:30:03.320
that we should
take into mind when

00:30:03.320 --> 00:30:10.290
deciding whether to go down
the VC financing path or not?

00:30:10.290 --> 00:30:13.035
And how does that affect
the day-to-day operations.

00:30:13.035 --> 00:30:14.660
I feel like I have
a hunch, but I would

00:30:14.660 --> 00:30:15.900
love to hear your thoughts.

00:30:15.900 --> 00:30:16.400
Yeah.

00:30:16.400 --> 00:30:18.080
It's a fantastic
question, and I think

00:30:18.080 --> 00:30:19.830
one that's probably
not asked enough,

00:30:19.830 --> 00:30:22.910
especially around a campus
like MIT where, or venture--

00:30:22.910 --> 00:30:24.650
frankly, VCs are
all over campus.

00:30:24.650 --> 00:30:27.620
And second, it's
fun to talk about.

00:30:27.620 --> 00:30:31.790
It feels like a huge milestone
to raise a big amount of money.

00:30:31.790 --> 00:30:34.357
And so the first thing I
will say about that is I

00:30:34.357 --> 00:30:35.690
think that's the wrong attitude.

00:30:35.690 --> 00:30:37.460
I think instead of
saying congratulations

00:30:37.460 --> 00:30:40.880
when somebody races around,
it should be good luck.

00:30:40.880 --> 00:30:42.860
Because basically what
you're signing up for,

00:30:42.860 --> 00:30:44.330
to start answering
your question,

00:30:44.330 --> 00:30:51.080
is you're signing up to return
a lot more in capital than you

00:30:51.080 --> 00:30:52.700
took.

00:30:52.700 --> 00:30:55.940
Venture is an asset class
where they don't want just two

00:30:55.940 --> 00:30:57.120
times their money back.

00:30:57.120 --> 00:31:00.160
They want 10x or
100x their money.

00:31:00.160 --> 00:31:04.580
So first of all, you have to be
ready to be on their timeline.

00:31:04.580 --> 00:31:07.280
Typically what they want you
to do is return their money.

00:31:07.280 --> 00:31:09.980
So that level of return
in five to seven years,

00:31:09.980 --> 00:31:13.590
or sometimes now more like
seven to 10 years timeline.

00:31:13.590 --> 00:31:18.800
So if you want to have a
hypergrowth scale for impact

00:31:18.800 --> 00:31:20.980
business-- and that scale
for impact is a big deal.

00:31:20.980 --> 00:31:23.480
That's why people do want to
take venture money, and they do

00:31:23.480 --> 00:31:24.890
want to become founders
a lot of the time,

00:31:24.890 --> 00:31:26.473
because they want
to have that impact.

00:31:26.473 --> 00:31:29.480
And you really do, capital
is your way to get there.

00:31:29.480 --> 00:31:31.670
To have money to grow
your business to a scale

00:31:31.670 --> 00:31:35.420
where not only are the
returns great for you

00:31:35.420 --> 00:31:37.610
and the investors, but the
impact of your business

00:31:37.610 --> 00:31:39.410
is great for your
customers and society

00:31:39.410 --> 00:31:42.220
depending on what
the business is.

00:31:42.220 --> 00:31:43.760
So the first thing
I would say is

00:31:43.760 --> 00:31:45.860
you have to be ready
to be on that timeline

00:31:45.860 --> 00:31:47.550
and that hypergrowth mentality.

00:31:47.550 --> 00:31:51.080
And so if you don't have those
fundamental questions answered

00:31:51.080 --> 00:31:53.600
in the business plan, for
instance, you're probably--

00:31:53.600 --> 00:31:57.650
you may or may not be ready
to be on that growth path.

00:31:57.650 --> 00:32:00.500
And then I think the
second thing is--

00:32:00.500 --> 00:32:03.530
so actually, so
personally to me,

00:32:03.530 --> 00:32:06.520
my business partner
just wasn't interested.

00:32:06.520 --> 00:32:08.270
We both came from
venture-backed startups.

00:32:08.270 --> 00:32:11.010
She's actually a full-time
career coach in her day job

00:32:11.010 --> 00:32:13.080
when mine is at MIT.

00:32:13.080 --> 00:32:15.080
And she said, I'm
really burnt out

00:32:15.080 --> 00:32:17.420
on that type of
business building.

00:32:17.420 --> 00:32:20.043
Maybe we'll do that one day,
but it's actually not something

00:32:20.043 --> 00:32:21.210
I'm interested in right now.

00:32:21.210 --> 00:32:24.710
So she made the decision for
us, and I was supportive of that

00:32:24.710 --> 00:32:28.400
just given the whole picture
of our lives right now.

00:32:28.400 --> 00:32:31.250
We didn't decide
to do that 100%.

00:32:31.250 --> 00:32:33.680
And so the second part
of that on the other side

00:32:33.680 --> 00:32:36.230
is, do you think your
business can return that?

00:32:36.230 --> 00:32:37.940
When you figure out
those unit economics

00:32:37.940 --> 00:32:40.100
and you figure out
that business model,

00:32:40.100 --> 00:32:42.655
is your business going to
have a venture-backed return?

00:32:42.655 --> 00:32:44.030
Because there's
a lot of startups

00:32:44.030 --> 00:32:47.420
that simply aren't, and
that's totally fine.

00:32:47.420 --> 00:32:49.940
But you should have an idea--

00:32:49.940 --> 00:32:52.342
not in detail-- but high level.

00:32:52.342 --> 00:32:54.300
How would I even go about
returning that money?

00:32:54.300 --> 00:32:56.540
And are the economics of
my business ever going

00:32:56.540 --> 00:33:00.560
to get to a point where I
could be having $100 million

00:33:00.560 --> 00:33:03.440
in revenue every month?

00:33:03.440 --> 00:33:05.840
Monthly recurring revenue
or annual recurring revenue.

00:33:05.840 --> 00:33:08.180
And if the answer is no,
then that's totally fine.

00:33:08.180 --> 00:33:10.940
An e-commerce business,
direct-to-customer,

00:33:10.940 --> 00:33:14.590
that's a business model that
has tipped a lot of times--

00:33:14.590 --> 00:33:15.920
not all the time--

00:33:15.920 --> 00:33:18.680
but has proven that
can return because they

00:33:18.680 --> 00:33:21.440
don't have heavy assets of
a brick and mortar store.

00:33:21.440 --> 00:33:24.940
That's what you're
trying to disrupt.

00:33:24.940 --> 00:33:27.790
But those businesses-- there's
some really big successes,

00:33:27.790 --> 00:33:31.480
and then sometimes there's
other challenges like logistics

00:33:31.480 --> 00:33:32.660
and that sort of stuff.

00:33:32.660 --> 00:33:34.960
So I hope that answers
your questions.

00:33:34.960 --> 00:33:35.860
It does, yeah, no.

00:33:35.860 --> 00:33:38.350
I really appreciate the
perspective, and I agree.

00:33:38.350 --> 00:33:40.990
Definitely not something
we talk about much,

00:33:40.990 --> 00:33:43.210
and I wish there was more
conversation around it.

00:33:43.210 --> 00:33:46.090
If you're at that point
where you're not quite

00:33:46.090 --> 00:33:50.020
a venture-backable business,
where [INAUDIBLE]----

00:33:50.020 --> 00:33:52.120
but you don't quite
have enough funding

00:33:52.120 --> 00:33:53.620
to continue going forward.

00:33:53.620 --> 00:33:56.140
What are the different
financing options

00:33:56.140 --> 00:33:57.460
available at that point?

00:33:57.460 --> 00:34:00.310
Yeah, so the best
source of financing

00:34:00.310 --> 00:34:02.290
always is revenue
from customers.

00:34:02.290 --> 00:34:05.410
So that's why focusing
on the customer

00:34:05.410 --> 00:34:07.930
early is so important
to us because,

00:34:07.930 --> 00:34:10.265
to have that option even--

00:34:10.265 --> 00:34:12.040
there's some great
stories from business--

00:34:12.040 --> 00:34:14.739
and I'd be happy to
send you one or two

00:34:14.739 --> 00:34:17.920
that I have in my
mind-- of companies

00:34:17.920 --> 00:34:22.360
bootstrapping until they're $10
million in monthly revenues.

00:34:22.360 --> 00:34:24.429
And then them
saying, OK, if I want

00:34:24.429 --> 00:34:26.889
to get past this I really
need some venture capital.

00:34:26.889 --> 00:34:29.290
And the whole idea
of venture capital

00:34:29.290 --> 00:34:32.590
is that you're trading ownership
in your company for money.

00:34:32.590 --> 00:34:35.600
And so if you can get
to a crazy milestone--

00:34:35.600 --> 00:34:37.600
I'm not saying that's
easy-- but if you actually

00:34:37.600 --> 00:34:40.270
have the capacity to raise
revenue from your customers

00:34:40.270 --> 00:34:43.540
or get in the medical
community or the deep science

00:34:43.540 --> 00:34:48.130
community like the NIH, or other
different grant opportunities.

00:34:48.130 --> 00:34:50.080
That is equity-free money.

00:34:50.080 --> 00:34:52.239
MIT there is a lot
of equity-free money.

00:34:52.239 --> 00:34:56.440
Not in the millions, but in
the tens of thousands for sure,

00:34:56.440 --> 00:35:01.300
if not $100,000 if you were
to win the 100K competition.

00:35:01.300 --> 00:35:04.510
The further you can go
before taking venture money,

00:35:04.510 --> 00:35:06.680
the more of your company
you will be able to own.

00:35:06.680 --> 00:35:09.310
So if you do have that really
crazy return on the other end,

00:35:09.310 --> 00:35:12.220
you can really profit from it as
opposed to just your investors.

00:35:17.478 --> 00:35:19.710
Yeah, all you have to do
is win this competition.

00:35:19.710 --> 00:35:21.801
That's the easiest path.

00:35:24.450 --> 00:35:29.000
Honestly, it's easier than going
through that whole headache

00:35:29.000 --> 00:35:31.990
of trying to raise venture
and being stuck with them.

00:35:31.990 --> 00:35:34.410
This is a little bit of pain
for a short amount of time.

00:35:34.410 --> 00:35:37.890
That's a lot of different
type of pain for a lot longer.

00:35:37.890 --> 00:35:42.060
So I've heard a few stories
like you can't divorce anyone

00:35:42.060 --> 00:35:44.210
who invests in your business.

00:35:44.210 --> 00:35:46.140
And so it's the
only relationship

00:35:46.140 --> 00:35:48.330
you can't get out of.

00:35:48.330 --> 00:35:50.220
One thing that I
think is interesting

00:35:50.220 --> 00:35:52.050
is writing a business
plan is often

00:35:52.050 --> 00:35:55.410
kind of a clarifying, sometimes
transformative, process

00:35:55.410 --> 00:35:56.520
for ideas.

00:35:56.520 --> 00:35:59.220
Do you have any examples
of a good before

00:35:59.220 --> 00:36:02.550
and after of an idea with going
through that business plan

00:36:02.550 --> 00:36:05.310
process?

00:36:05.310 --> 00:36:06.340
It's a good question.

00:36:06.340 --> 00:36:08.920
I don't have one
that comes to mind.

00:36:08.920 --> 00:36:11.250
But I think a common
thing that happens

00:36:11.250 --> 00:36:15.540
is people are not specific
enough with their customer

00:36:15.540 --> 00:36:18.190
in particular or the way
they want to go to market.

00:36:18.190 --> 00:36:21.960
So even saying, oh we're
a consumer business.

00:36:21.960 --> 00:36:23.875
We're going to go and
do digital advertising.

00:36:23.875 --> 00:36:25.500
I hear that from
students all the time.

00:36:25.500 --> 00:36:27.060
And I'm like, great.

00:36:27.060 --> 00:36:29.100
Tell me more about that
digital advertising.

00:36:29.100 --> 00:36:31.650
What's the cost of
customer acquisition?

00:36:31.650 --> 00:36:33.720
What is the cost per
click of your ad?

00:36:33.720 --> 00:36:35.470
What does that funnel look like?

00:36:35.470 --> 00:36:38.430
And so I think that the
transformation that typically

00:36:38.430 --> 00:36:42.600
happens is you think you have
a level of specificity that's

00:36:42.600 --> 00:36:45.810
enough, but in reality when
you actually go to write it

00:36:45.810 --> 00:36:49.560
out, or go to try to communicate
to somebody that sort of is

00:36:49.560 --> 00:36:51.780
in a position to give
you real feedback,

00:36:51.780 --> 00:36:54.060
it's not nearly specific enough.

00:36:54.060 --> 00:36:56.520
So I think it really
helps to narrow in.

00:36:56.520 --> 00:36:58.440
I think that's really
good advice, especially

00:36:58.440 --> 00:37:00.180
since we have such
a heavy mentorship

00:37:00.180 --> 00:37:02.110
component in this competition.

00:37:02.110 --> 00:37:05.790
And if people want to take
the most advantage, maximize

00:37:05.790 --> 00:37:09.660
their time with people, having
greater specificity to then get

00:37:09.660 --> 00:37:13.170
feedback on that is a lot better
than taking someone's time

00:37:13.170 --> 00:37:16.282
as they walk you
through that process.

00:37:16.282 --> 00:37:17.490
Wherever you are, it's great.

00:37:17.490 --> 00:37:20.640
If you need to help finding
that specificity, we're here.

00:37:20.640 --> 00:37:23.370
But if you have the capacity
for that ahead of time,

00:37:23.370 --> 00:37:26.760
you can really maximize the
mentorship opportunities.

00:37:26.760 --> 00:37:28.140
Yeah, and I would add to that.

00:37:28.140 --> 00:37:29.730
I think that's totally
right, Shannon.

00:37:29.730 --> 00:37:32.010
And people, especially
mentors, they

00:37:32.010 --> 00:37:33.900
want to brainstorm with you.

00:37:33.900 --> 00:37:37.380
They want to actually help
you, and they can't help you

00:37:37.380 --> 00:37:39.690
if you're just having-- if
the whole conversation is

00:37:39.690 --> 00:37:42.060
about getting you to
explain what you're doing.

00:37:42.060 --> 00:37:44.280
If you actually
present them with,

00:37:44.280 --> 00:37:47.342
I tested this, A versus B,
and this is what happened.

00:37:47.342 --> 00:37:49.800
Then they're going to be like,
oh that's such a great test.

00:37:49.800 --> 00:37:52.920
And oh, tell me what
you learned, and here's

00:37:52.920 --> 00:37:54.600
five other ideas
of other things you

00:37:54.600 --> 00:37:58.285
can test as opposed to
you starting to say, oh,

00:37:58.285 --> 00:37:59.910
I'm going to have a
digital marketing--

00:37:59.910 --> 00:38:02.730
I saw the industry averages.

00:38:02.730 --> 00:38:05.713
$15 cost per click, and
it's like, OK, great.

00:38:05.713 --> 00:38:07.630
That doesn't really tell
me anything specific.

00:38:07.630 --> 00:38:09.713
It doesn't give me an idea
of your thought process

00:38:09.713 --> 00:38:12.210
as an entrepreneur or executor.

00:38:12.210 --> 00:38:13.817
You've done some
basic market research

00:38:13.817 --> 00:38:15.150
and have some basic assumptions.

00:38:15.150 --> 00:38:17.880
But to really go the
extra mile and hopefully

00:38:17.880 --> 00:38:19.560
your business plan
can sort of reveal

00:38:19.560 --> 00:38:21.840
when you need to do
that, will really

00:38:21.840 --> 00:38:24.120
help you have much more
productive conversations

00:38:24.120 --> 00:38:26.940
and utilize the resources
of this competition

00:38:26.940 --> 00:38:30.250
and the other resources around
MIT a little bit better.

00:38:30.250 --> 00:38:32.880
So in that same
vein, what happens

00:38:32.880 --> 00:38:33.930
if you change your mind?

00:38:33.930 --> 00:38:36.900
What happens if you need
to evolve your idea?

00:38:36.900 --> 00:38:38.640
What happens if
you're not growing?

00:38:38.640 --> 00:38:41.040
Or if you miscalculated
your risks?

00:38:41.040 --> 00:38:43.300
How do you change your
existing infrastructure?

00:38:43.300 --> 00:38:45.990
Especially in response
to either failure

00:38:45.990 --> 00:38:48.480
or constructive
criticism from a mentor,

00:38:48.480 --> 00:38:49.920
or whatever it might be.

00:38:49.920 --> 00:38:50.430
Yeah.

00:38:50.430 --> 00:38:54.270
So that obviously gets
more difficult over time,

00:38:54.270 --> 00:38:56.700
but it's never too late.

00:38:56.700 --> 00:38:59.130
I will use the big
tech story of last week

00:38:59.130 --> 00:39:00.430
to give everybody an example.

00:39:00.430 --> 00:39:04.110
So slack, which just sold to
Salesforce for $26 billion,

00:39:04.110 --> 00:39:08.110
was a video game application.

00:39:08.110 --> 00:39:08.895
And I think--

00:39:08.895 --> 00:39:10.860
I don't know the number off
the top of my head of venture

00:39:10.860 --> 00:39:12.120
capital that they
burned, but they

00:39:12.120 --> 00:39:13.287
were a full-fledged company.

00:39:13.287 --> 00:39:15.837
And by the way, Stewart
Butterfield, who is the CEO,

00:39:15.837 --> 00:39:16.920
this was his second rodeo.

00:39:16.920 --> 00:39:19.500
So it's not like he wasn't
experienced as an entrepreneur

00:39:19.500 --> 00:39:21.540
and an executor.

00:39:21.540 --> 00:39:23.320
He had built Flickr
before, which I

00:39:23.320 --> 00:39:24.570
believe was a different pivot.

00:39:24.570 --> 00:39:27.210
I don't know if you guys
are old enough to remember.

00:39:27.210 --> 00:39:30.630
Flickr which was a photo
app that sold to Yahoo.

00:39:30.630 --> 00:39:33.570
It was one of the
original photo apps,

00:39:33.570 --> 00:39:37.020
and he made a second
pivot with Slack.

00:39:37.020 --> 00:39:38.730
It was literally
a video game app.

00:39:38.730 --> 00:39:42.150
Slack was a chat, an internal
messaging tool that they used.

00:39:42.150 --> 00:39:45.900
And then they decided to get
all their investors together,

00:39:45.900 --> 00:39:48.210
tell them he wasn't going
to return their money,

00:39:48.210 --> 00:39:50.658
he was going to reinvest the
money in this new business

00:39:50.658 --> 00:39:52.200
and he hoped that
they were on board.

00:39:52.200 --> 00:39:53.700
I can't remember
if they gave people

00:39:53.700 --> 00:39:57.030
an opportunity to buy
out or not at that point.

00:39:57.030 --> 00:40:00.030
And obviously that
was a great success.

00:40:00.030 --> 00:40:02.370
That's extreme, I'm not
saying that's your pivot.

00:40:02.370 --> 00:40:06.600
But I think to try to answer
your question more literally

00:40:06.600 --> 00:40:11.670
and more something that people
can actually take away from it

00:40:11.670 --> 00:40:14.670
is, you need to--

00:40:14.670 --> 00:40:16.650
the first thing is,
you need to figure out

00:40:16.650 --> 00:40:18.900
again the specific things
you want to change,

00:40:18.900 --> 00:40:21.130
and you need to be able
to communicate that.

00:40:21.130 --> 00:40:24.450
So I think the most important
thing, and what I'm sure

00:40:24.450 --> 00:40:28.200
Stewart did right, was that he
was able to communicate exactly

00:40:28.200 --> 00:40:31.290
why what he was doing
wasn't working anymore

00:40:31.290 --> 00:40:32.760
and where he was going.

00:40:32.760 --> 00:40:33.900
He had a new vision.

00:40:33.900 --> 00:40:35.400
He had a new mission statement.

00:40:35.400 --> 00:40:37.770
He had a new business plan.

00:40:37.770 --> 00:40:40.120
And so I'm sure what
he was able to do

00:40:40.120 --> 00:40:42.870
was very effectively communicate
that not only to his investors,

00:40:42.870 --> 00:40:47.100
who he needed to, in theory,
get on board, to his board

00:40:47.100 --> 00:40:49.770
members, who I'm sure
had to approve the pivot,

00:40:49.770 --> 00:40:52.140
and then to his
employees, who he

00:40:52.140 --> 00:40:55.153
was going to change the
entire purpose of their job.

00:40:55.153 --> 00:40:57.570
And they were no longer working
at a gaming company, which

00:40:57.570 --> 00:41:00.360
is certainly a specific type
of culture of a startup,

00:41:00.360 --> 00:41:04.530
to an enterprise chat solution.

00:41:04.530 --> 00:41:07.860
Which is a really different
type of company to build.

00:41:07.860 --> 00:41:10.440
So I don't think anybody should
ever be scared of pivoting.

00:41:13.050 --> 00:41:16.440
I get students who are four or
five months into their venture

00:41:16.440 --> 00:41:18.780
they say, you know,
this isn't working.

00:41:18.780 --> 00:41:20.582
And then they just keep
bumping against it,

00:41:20.582 --> 00:41:21.540
but they're too scared.

00:41:21.540 --> 00:41:23.582
And it's like, well, I
already put this much time

00:41:23.582 --> 00:41:24.840
into my business.

00:41:24.840 --> 00:41:26.400
There's so much more
time, hopefully,

00:41:26.400 --> 00:41:29.220
on the other side
of this decision.

00:41:29.220 --> 00:41:32.160
So whatever-- I think one of
the most frustrating parts

00:41:32.160 --> 00:41:35.160
about entrepreneurship is
that it's not efficient,

00:41:35.160 --> 00:41:37.590
and the sunken
costs are something

00:41:37.590 --> 00:41:40.230
that's really hard
for us to let go of.

00:41:40.230 --> 00:41:44.130
And I think that's actually one
of the most important muscles

00:41:44.130 --> 00:41:47.040
that you can build in early
stage startups is learning how

00:41:47.040 --> 00:41:49.800
to be OK with saying goodbye
to your sunken costs,

00:41:49.800 --> 00:41:51.810
with saying goodbye
to the time you just

00:41:51.810 --> 00:41:54.630
wasted trying a bunch of
stuff that didn't work.

00:41:54.630 --> 00:41:57.313
And it's that analogy--

00:41:57.313 --> 00:41:58.980
I don't have a better
analogy than this,

00:41:58.980 --> 00:42:00.897
but it's what people say
when you have a baby.

00:42:00.897 --> 00:42:04.320
You forget all the pain a minute
after you see your child's face

00:42:04.320 --> 00:42:05.350
for the first time.

00:42:05.350 --> 00:42:06.210
It's the same thing.

00:42:06.210 --> 00:42:09.120
You could-- I have students
who they've made 10 pivots.

00:42:09.120 --> 00:42:10.350
I run delta v in New York.

00:42:10.350 --> 00:42:13.170
We pivot all summer
sometimes for teams.

00:42:13.170 --> 00:42:16.680
I had a team in 2018
called [? Rune. ?]

00:42:16.680 --> 00:42:20.610
They started as a
customer service app.

00:42:20.610 --> 00:42:22.830
They had natural
language processing,

00:42:22.830 --> 00:42:23.820
and so they decided--

00:42:23.820 --> 00:42:25.362
they came into delta
v, they pitch me

00:42:25.362 --> 00:42:27.420
on this awesome idea I
was super excited about,

00:42:27.420 --> 00:42:29.580
helping to train customer
service agents a lot

00:42:29.580 --> 00:42:31.560
more fast and efficiently.

00:42:31.560 --> 00:42:35.100
And they pivoted for two
months into the program

00:42:35.100 --> 00:42:38.010
and didn't land on their
existing idea till August 5th.

00:42:38.010 --> 00:42:42.060
Delta v is a three month
program, it ends August 30.

00:42:42.060 --> 00:42:44.370
And that was really
well worth it.

00:42:44.370 --> 00:42:46.320
they did such an amazing job.

00:42:46.320 --> 00:42:51.390
And 2 and 1/2 years later
they went through YC,

00:42:51.390 --> 00:42:53.130
they raised $2 million,
and they're still

00:42:53.130 --> 00:42:54.338
in business in San Francisco.

00:42:54.338 --> 00:42:56.280
So even though they
might have felt

00:42:56.280 --> 00:42:58.890
like they had, at that point,
six months of sunken costs,

00:42:58.890 --> 00:43:02.490
it was it was an unbelievable
reflection on them

00:43:02.490 --> 00:43:05.500
as entrepreneurs to see that
they were willing to pivot.

00:43:05.500 --> 00:43:07.500
And this isn't the most
comfortable environment.

00:43:07.500 --> 00:43:09.060
They had board meetings
they had to go to.

00:43:09.060 --> 00:43:10.477
They had mentors
they were telling

00:43:10.477 --> 00:43:12.360
what they did all summer.

00:43:12.360 --> 00:43:14.310
And they just stood
there proudly and said,

00:43:14.310 --> 00:43:15.180
you know, we're pivoting.

00:43:15.180 --> 00:43:15.847
What can we say.

00:43:15.847 --> 00:43:17.287
We're not going
to nail it until--

00:43:17.287 --> 00:43:18.870
we're not going to
do this until we're

00:43:18.870 --> 00:43:21.790
super excited about what we're
doing, and it really paid off.

00:43:21.790 --> 00:43:25.080
And I have a lot
of the stories too.

00:43:25.080 --> 00:43:28.900
That is really-- you've got
to have a little bit of nerve

00:43:28.900 --> 00:43:31.257
to pivot late in the
process like that.

00:43:31.257 --> 00:43:32.840
But I think that's
a good point, and I

00:43:32.840 --> 00:43:35.940
think that the Slack
example was really poignant.

00:43:35.940 --> 00:43:37.537
This has been a great--

00:43:37.537 --> 00:43:39.120
an hour is definitely
not enough time,

00:43:39.120 --> 00:43:41.310
and there's so much talk
about entrepreneurship.

00:43:41.310 --> 00:43:44.652
But if you have one takeaway
or one pearl of wisdom

00:43:44.652 --> 00:43:46.110
to share with folks
who are looking

00:43:46.110 --> 00:43:49.630
to compete in this creative arts
competition or entrepreneurship

00:43:49.630 --> 00:43:51.520
competitions more broadly.

00:43:51.520 --> 00:43:55.470
What would that key takeaway
or pro of wisdom be?

00:43:55.470 --> 00:43:57.818
Yeah, I'm going to sound
like a broken record,

00:43:57.818 --> 00:43:59.610
but it's really your
ability to communicate

00:43:59.610 --> 00:44:00.900
is the most important thing.

00:44:00.900 --> 00:44:04.000
You can have the coolest idea in
the world, the coolest product,

00:44:04.000 --> 00:44:06.660
but if you cannot communicate
that to others and you

00:44:06.660 --> 00:44:08.140
can't get people
excited about it,

00:44:08.140 --> 00:44:11.670
or ultimately buy your
product, it doesn't matter.

00:44:11.670 --> 00:44:13.200
There are so many
cool technologies

00:44:13.200 --> 00:44:16.260
that never got off the
ground, or cool technologies

00:44:16.260 --> 00:44:18.117
that where the
inventor didn't end up

00:44:18.117 --> 00:44:19.950
getting credit because
they weren't the ones

00:44:19.950 --> 00:44:21.510
to commercialize it.

00:44:21.510 --> 00:44:27.270
The MP3 being one of the
best examples in history.

00:44:27.270 --> 00:44:31.110
And so not only should
you focus on communication

00:44:31.110 --> 00:44:33.120
and it shouldn't be a
second thought, it really

00:44:33.120 --> 00:44:35.940
should be one of
your primary focuses,

00:44:35.940 --> 00:44:38.610
just like technology and
product development is

00:44:38.610 --> 00:44:41.730
for so many of us at MIT.

00:44:41.730 --> 00:44:46.170
Practicing communication is
just the most important thing.

00:44:46.170 --> 00:44:47.670
And not only for
this competition

00:44:47.670 --> 00:44:50.280
that I know, but
really in the long run.

00:44:50.280 --> 00:44:53.400
And figuring out not only
what you're going to say,

00:44:53.400 --> 00:44:55.020
but how you're going
to deliver it--

00:44:55.020 --> 00:44:58.330
equally important.

00:44:58.330 --> 00:44:59.770
I have a question.

00:44:59.770 --> 00:45:02.600
Sure.

00:45:02.600 --> 00:45:03.745
What would be your opinion?

00:45:06.880 --> 00:45:11.965
Is there an important difference
between our focused businesses

00:45:11.965 --> 00:45:14.770
and other types of
businesses such as technology

00:45:14.770 --> 00:45:16.120
focused ones?

00:45:16.120 --> 00:45:21.370
And the other question
would be, what

00:45:21.370 --> 00:45:25.210
would be the key challenges
for art projects?

00:45:25.210 --> 00:45:31.180
And how can we face them?

00:45:31.180 --> 00:45:33.095
Yeah, that's a great question.

00:45:33.095 --> 00:45:34.720
So the first thing
I will say is that I

00:45:34.720 --> 00:45:40.870
think creative arts businesses
can be technology businesses.

00:45:40.870 --> 00:45:43.960
So they can be two
different things, but they--

00:45:43.960 --> 00:45:46.030
for the purpose, especially
this competition,

00:45:46.030 --> 00:45:47.830
they're one and the same.

00:45:47.830 --> 00:45:49.790
Or they need to be combined.

00:45:49.790 --> 00:45:53.260
And then, frankly, one of the
challenges with this sector

00:45:53.260 --> 00:45:56.950
overall is that in a way,
there's so much opportunity

00:45:56.950 --> 00:45:59.380
because there's been so many
business models and arts that

00:45:59.380 --> 00:46:03.430
have been really challenged
since the internet

00:46:03.430 --> 00:46:05.780
when you think about
music in particular.

00:46:05.780 --> 00:46:07.780
One of my first things I
was thinking about when

00:46:07.780 --> 00:46:11.290
you answered that question
is, the size of the market

00:46:11.290 --> 00:46:14.920
sometimes isn't as big in arts
as it is in other industries,

00:46:14.920 --> 00:46:17.890
but then I think about
Spotify and the music market.

00:46:17.890 --> 00:46:20.428
And I was just listening to
somebody talk about this.

00:46:20.428 --> 00:46:22.720
And I think it was Kara
Swisher, who I'm a huge fan of,

00:46:22.720 --> 00:46:24.220
she's a technology reporter.

00:46:24.220 --> 00:46:28.558
And she was talking about how
in no other medium has there

00:46:28.558 --> 00:46:30.100
been such an elegant
solution to have

00:46:30.100 --> 00:46:31.630
every single song in the world.

00:46:31.630 --> 00:46:33.940
You can't have every TV show
on a single app right now.

00:46:33.940 --> 00:46:36.610
You can't-- I guess you
can have every book,

00:46:36.610 --> 00:46:39.760
but with audible
and some other--

00:46:39.760 --> 00:46:41.710
I don't even know what
the book apps are,

00:46:41.710 --> 00:46:46.030
but Spotify was such a feat.

00:46:46.030 --> 00:46:47.620
But it's difficult, right?

00:46:47.620 --> 00:46:51.340
And I think creative arts
like every other industry

00:46:51.340 --> 00:46:55.150
is getting eaten by the
really big tech giants.

00:46:55.150 --> 00:46:57.970
And so everybody now is
trying to play in music.

00:46:57.970 --> 00:47:07.092
Apple, Amazon, Google, and
it's a really interesting time

00:47:07.092 --> 00:47:08.050
to be in creative arts.

00:47:08.050 --> 00:47:12.040
I think even now with COVID
you see all the theater

00:47:12.040 --> 00:47:14.560
artists in particular
having to completely rethink

00:47:14.560 --> 00:47:17.080
the entire way that
they do things.

00:47:17.080 --> 00:47:21.250
I think there's a lot of
challenge in this area

00:47:21.250 --> 00:47:23.620
and in this industry,
and in a way that's

00:47:23.620 --> 00:47:25.540
the most exciting thing
for entrepreneurs,

00:47:25.540 --> 00:47:28.150
to see all the
opportunity and try

00:47:28.150 --> 00:47:32.693
to wade through all the mess
that's happening right now.

00:47:32.693 --> 00:47:34.360
So hopefully it's a
really exciting year

00:47:34.360 --> 00:47:37.000
in particular to really
be innovating in the arts.

00:47:39.950 --> 00:47:41.930
Thank you.

00:47:41.930 --> 00:47:43.310
All right.

00:47:43.310 --> 00:47:47.200
I could probably talk to you
for 16 more years about Covid

00:47:47.200 --> 00:47:49.400
innovation in the
arts, but that's not

00:47:49.400 --> 00:47:51.290
why we're here tonight.

00:47:51.290 --> 00:47:53.050
If anybody has any
other questions

00:47:53.050 --> 00:47:55.880
I would like to encourage that.

00:47:55.880 --> 00:47:59.460
And if not, thank
you so much, Carly.

00:47:59.460 --> 00:48:01.070
This was really fantastic.

00:48:01.070 --> 00:48:03.950
Great conversation,
great presentation.

00:48:03.950 --> 00:48:05.450
I really appreciate
your being here.

00:48:05.450 --> 00:48:07.340
And thank you to
everybody else who came.

00:48:07.340 --> 00:48:09.690
Really great questions,
very engaging.

00:48:09.690 --> 00:48:10.850
This was a delight.

00:48:10.850 --> 00:48:11.780
Yeah, thank you guys.

00:48:11.780 --> 00:48:15.400
This is really fun, and thanks
for all the great questions.

